+# Pre-ship Review — USDC Payouts dApp (Base)
+
+Scope: frontend (`packages/nextjs`) + its config. Report only; no code changed.
+Contract (`Payouts.sol`) reviewed for context only; it is deployed and out of scope for changes.
+
+---
+
+## Ship-blocking
+
+### SB1 — Writes bypass SE-2 target-network guard: txs can fire on the wrong chain
+**File:** `app/payouts/page.tsx:21`, `:36–62` (uses raw `useWriteContract` from wagmi)
+
+`handleApprove`/`handlePay` call wagmi's raw `useWriteContract`, which submits to
+**whatever chain the wallet is currently on** — there is no chain check, no network-switch
+prompt, and no simulation. Meanwhile the balance read (`useScaffoldReadContract`) is pinned
+to Base (8453) regardless of wallet chain, so the UI shows the correct Base balance while a
+tx can be broadcast on Ethereum mainnet or any other connected chain. On the wrong chain the
+hardcoded Base addresses (`0x8335…2913` USDC, `0x7c3f…b6F2` Payouts) point at unrelated/
+nonexistent contracts — user pays gas for a failed or unintended tx, with real fund-loss risk.
+
+`useScaffoldWriteContract` already handles all of this (wrong-network error, pre-flight
+`simulateContractWriteAndNotifyError`, `useTransactor` toasts + block-confirmation), and the
+project rules mandate it.
+
+**Fix:** replace both raw writes with `useScaffoldWriteContract`:
+```ts
+const { writeContractAsync: writePayouts, isMining: payPending } =
+  useScaffoldWriteContract({ contractName: "Payouts" });
+const { writeContractAsync: writeUsdc, isMining: approvePending } =
+  useScaffoldWriteContract({ contractName: "USDC" });
+// approve:
+await writeUsdc({ functionName: "approve", args: [PAYOUTS.address, parseUnits(amount || "0", 6)] });
+// pay:
+await writePayouts({ functionName: "pay", args: [recipient, parseUnits(amount || "0", 6), memo] });
+```
+This removes the raw `abi`/`address` plumbing and gives per-button `isMining` (see SF3).
+
+### SB2 — Event feed scans from genesis on Base mainnet
+**File:** `app/payouts/page.tsx:32` (`fromBlock: 0n`)
+
+`useScaffoldEventHistory` defaults `fromBlock` to the contract's `deployedOnBlock`
+(`33712450`, see `deployedContracts.ts:48`), but the page overrides it with `0n`, forcing a
+scan of ~33.7M blocks via `getLogs` on every poll. On Base mainnet through the shared Alchemy
+key this is extremely slow, burns rate limit, and will frequently error — the "Latest payouts"
+feed effectively won't load for real users.
+
+**Fix:** drop the `fromBlock` override so it uses `deployedOnBlock`, or set it explicitly:
+```ts
+useScaffoldEventHistory({ contractName: "Payouts", eventName: "Paid", watch: true });
+```
+Note: `useScaffoldEventHistory` is `@deprecated` for production (getLogs overloads L2 RPCs).
+For a feed shipping to real users, plan to move to an indexer (ponder / subgraph) — see SF5.
+
+### SB3 — Production ships on shared demo API keys
+**File:** `scaffold.config.ts:16`, `:29`, `:40`; `.env.example`
+
+`DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY = "IZYEU2cWBgnFmgiTAgpWD"` and the default WalletConnect
+`walletConnectProjectId = "3a8170812b534d0ff9d794f19a901d64"` are Scaffold-ETH's shared
+public defaults. With `pollingInterval` watching events + balances on Base mainnet for real
+users, the shared Alchemy key will be throttled (compounding SB2), and the shared WC project
+id is not domain-allowlisted — WalletConnect connections/analytics will be unreliable.
+`.env.example` ships with both vars blank, so nothing forces them to be set before deploy.
+
+**Fix:** create app-owned Alchemy + WalletConnect keys and set
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY` / `NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID` in the Vercel/system
+env before shipping. Confirm they are present in the production environment (not just
+`.env.local`).
+
+---
+
+## Should-fix
+
+### SF1 — Recipient address is unvalidated and unsafely cast
+**File:** `app/payouts/page.tsx:55`, `:78–84`
+
+`recipient` is a free-text `<input>` cast with `recipient as \`0x${string}\``. A malformed or
+empty value reaches viem encoding and throws; the catch only `console.error`s (SF3), so the
+button appears to do nothing. No ENS support.
+
+**Fix:** use the SE-2 `AddressInput` from `@scaffold-ui/components` (already a dependency) and
+guard the action with viem's `isAddress`; disable "Send Payout" until valid:
+```tsx
+import { AddressInput } from "@scaffold-ui/components";
+import { isAddress } from "viem";
+<AddressInput value={recipient} onChange={setRecipient} placeholder="Recipient address" />
+// disabled={!isAddress(recipient) || !(Number(amount) > 0) || pending}
+```
+
+### SF2 — No user-facing error/success feedback; fields cleared before confirmation
+**File:** `app/payouts/page.tsx:44–46`, `:57–61`
+
+Both handlers swallow errors to `console.error` — a failed approve/pay is invisible to the
+user. `handlePay` also clears `amount`/`memo` immediately after `writeContractAsync` resolves
+(tx *submitted*, not confirmed), so the form resets even if the tx later reverts.
+
+**Fix:** adopting `useScaffoldWriteContract` (SB1) provides success/error toasts via
+`useTransactor`. For any remaining catch, surface `notification.error(getParsedError(e))` from
+`~~/utils/scaffold-eth`. Clear fields in the `onBlockConfirmation` callback (or after the await
+resolves *and* only on success), not optimistically.
+
+### SF3 — Approval UX: allowance never read; shared `isPending` disables both buttons
+**File:** `app/payouts/page.tsx:21`, `:99–113`
+
+The current allowance is never read, so users can't tell whether "Approve" is needed or
+whether they already approved — and approve is hardcoded to the exact `amount`, so any later
+higher payout silently needs re-approval. A single `isPending` from one write hook disables and
+shows `loading` on *both* buttons during either action.
+
+**Fix:** read allowance and drive the UI from it:
+```ts
+const { data: allowance } = useScaffoldReadContract({
+  contractName: "USDC", functionName: "allowance",
+  args: [connectedAddress, PAYOUTS.address],
+});
+```
+Show "Approve" only when `allowance < parseUnits(amount, 6)`; otherwise show "Send Payout".
+With two `useScaffoldWriteContract` hooks (SB1) each button gets its own `isMining`.
+
+### SF4 — Amount input has no validation
+**File:** `app/payouts/page.tsx:85–91`, `:42`, `:55`
+
+`type="number"` allows negatives / scientific notation (negative → `parseUnits` throws;
+caught silently), and >6 decimals are silently rounded by `parseUnits`. Nothing checks the
+amount against `usdcBalance` or enforces `> 0`, so users routinely hit the contract's
+`"zero amount"` / transfer-fails reverts.
+
+**Fix:** validate `Number(amount) > 0` and `parseUnits(amount,6) <= usdcBalance` before
+enabling the button; prefer SE-2's `IntegerInput`/`EtherInput`-style numeric input or constrain
+decimals to 6. Show a clear inline "Insufficient balance" state.
+
+### SF5 — Event feed: slow polling + misleading order/labeling for a live payout feed
+**File:** `scaffold.config.ts:45` (`pollingInterval: 30000`); `app/payouts/page.tsx:118`, `:120–121`
+
+`pollingInterval` is 30s (the SE-2 default is 4s; AGENTS.md says *decrease* it for L2). After a
+user sends a payout, "Latest payouts" can lag up to 30s, reading as a broken confirmation.
+`getLogs` returns oldest→newest, so under the "Latest payouts" heading the newest entry renders
+last, and `key={i}` (array index) is fragile as the list grows/reorders.
+
+**Fix:** lower `pollingInterval` (e.g. 4000–8000) balanced against the app-owned Alchemy key
+(SB3); render the feed newest-first (reverse before map) or relabel; key by
+`` `${payout.transactionHash}-${payout.logIndex}` ``. For production scale, migrate the feed to
+an indexer (ponder/subgraph) per the hook's deprecation note.
+
+---
+
+## Notes / nits (non-blocking)
+
+- `scaffold.config.ts:25,36` comments read "This is ours Alchemy's/WalletConnect's default" —
+  grammar aside, they misdescribe shared keys as the app's own; tidy when addressing SB3.
+- `app/payouts/page.tsx:10–11` index `deployedContracts[8453]` directly; if `targetNetworks`
+  ever changes this breaks at build. Low risk while Base-only, but the SE-2 hooks (SB1) already
+  resolve address/abi by name+network — using them removes the hardcode.
+- Consider showing the connected address via the SE-2 `Address` component and balance via
+  `Balance` for consistency with the rest of the app.

